  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective, transparent and impartial labour inspection and  harmonious industrial relations in place</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Malaysian government continues the engagement with ILO on the labour law reforms and to strengthen its institutions to implement the revised laws, including through its labour inspection system.

Improving workplace compliance through labour inspection in Malaysia has a critical link with work under the revision of legislation and reforms to the industrial relations system.

It further needs to seek opportunities to strengthen collaboration with relevant government agencies in addition to workers, employers and their organizations, to better understand the compliance gaps and to leverage their voice in community outreach and awareness raising, particularly with respect to the new laws and regulations that are expected to be adopted. 

Government capacity and effectiveness with regard to labour inspection faces a number of challenges related to coordinatedion of inspection services between the Labour Department; DOSH and SOCSO, strategic planning, in the efficient use of existing personnel and in deploying targeted inspection campaigns. Inspection planning could be improved, and the capacity of inspectors through effective sanctions to genuinely enforce the law is not clear. Given the key role that inspectors will play in ensuring compliance with the new legislation, it is essential to help the Government build its inspection capacity through a comprehensive program of strategic planning, training, strengthening enforcement capacity, and review of inspection tools and checklists.

The major aim is to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of labour administration, including labour inspections, to ensure that the new and amended laws, decrees or regulations, and other applicable legal instruments including collective agreements are being enforced in workplaces. 

Key reportable results include: Action Plan for Decent Work in the Rubber Glove 
Supply Chain; Codes of Conduct for rubber glove employers, and employment promoters for the rubber industry.</narrative>
  </description>
